// ── THE IN-BAND RECOVERY ACCESS ROUTE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE GAP THIS MODULE CLOSES. gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_secure answers AccessDiscover through
// BmcSecure, which needs a credential -- either a managed secret we hold or the published factory
// one. gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_rotation_route rotates FROM a working credential. So a board whose
// Administrator password is unknown has no route at all: every existing arm asks, first, for the
// thing that is missing. This module models the one route that starts without it -- reach the
// machine's own host OS, drive the BMC from inside the chassis over the in-band system interface (SSIF), actuate a factory reset --
// and it terminates by handing the factory credential to the EXISTING bmc_secure / bmc_onboard
// factory-login-then-rotate path.
//
// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT. Ruled with the cut-2 owner (gunbc.host.host_control_route /
// gunbc.host.bmc_control_binding, PR #14063): this is not a control route and not a third arm of
// HostControlRoute. The arms there answer "how do we drive this host's controller out-of-band"; this
// answers "how do we get the controller back", and it does not terminate in a binding -- it
// terminates in a BmcSecureStanding, and the ordinary join mints the binding. This module imports
// gunbc.host.bmc_control_binding nowhere, mints no binding, and mints no credential store. The
// dependency is one-way and short: this module -> BmcSecureStanding -> the control-route join.
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING FACT IS THE HOST OS, NOT THE MEDIUM (DESIGN §4d). Recovery is possible only
// because a rescue OS is running on the board and can reach /dev/ipmi0. Whether that OS arrived on a
// USB stick or over the network is a realisation of that ONE fact, so nothing here is named for USB:
// the precondition is RescueHostReachability, and media are providers of it -- one executed, one
// declared. Without a reachable rescue host there is no route, and this module says so with a named
// cause rather than modelling a recovery that needs somebody to walk to the machine.

// ── THE MEASURED FIRMWARE POLICY THAT MAKES THE RESET NECESSARY ────────────────────────────────
//
// THE FACT THAT DECIDES THE ACTUATION, AND WHERE IT LIVES. Writing the published factory password
// to the Administrator account is refused by the firmware we measured -- completion code 0xCC,
// "Invalid data field in request" -- on both the 8-byte and the 20-byte form, which is the
// observation that rules password length out as the explanation. A refused Set User Password changes
// nothing, so the unit stays locked: the route cannot be "write the factory password, log in", it has
// to be a factory reset.
//
// That refusal is a property of ONE VENDOR BUILD, so it is not stated here (and not in
// extdeps.bmc.openbmc either, which would make it a rule about OpenBMC in general). It is a per-
// release row in extdeps.boards.asrock_rack, keyed by the catalog's own typed BmcFirmwareVersion
// beside the release's other capabilities, and this module only ever ASKS for the release it is
// looking at: asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_for. A release the catalog
// lacks answers NotMeasured, and NotMeasured makes the route REFUSE (DESIGN §4d -- "we never read
// this firmware" is not a licence to assert recovery is possible). The completion code arrives as a
// typed value from the IPMI specification's completion-code list (extdeps.bmc.ipmi), with the English
// as its description: a free string would let "Request data length invalid" (0xC7), a complaint about
// the request LENGTH, be cited as the policy refusal and license a destructive ResetAll on a unit
// whose firmware would have accepted the write.

fn recovery_firmware_requires_reset(firmware: BmcFirmwareVersion) -> Bool {
  recovery_reset_is_authorized_by_measurement(
    policy: asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_for(firmware: firmware),
  )
}

fn recovery_measured_refusal_policy() -> PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy {
  asrock_altrad8ud_openbmc_3_22_00_published_factory_password_write_policy
}

// ── STEP 1: THE RESCUE MEDIUM, AS A PROVIDER OF THE REACHABLE HOST ─────────────────────────────
//
// The USB stick is not the precondition; the running rescue OS is. The medium is recorded because it
// is what makes that OS exist, and because the two ways of getting one are NOT at the same rung: the
// USB rescue has been executed and captured (an operator inserted a prepared Ubuntu live USB and the
// host took DHCP at its address); network boot has never served a real host --
// gunbc.network_boot_delivery says so of itself. Modelling both as equal would let a PXE path that
// has never delivered a kernel ground a recovery, which is exactly the fabricated-possibility class
// DESIGN §4d refuses. So a declared provider admits nothing: it can never satisfy
// RescueHostReachable, and its refusal is named SEPARATELY from "there is no rescue host", so a
// reader can tell "we have not built this provider" from "this unit has no way in".
type RescueHostMedium
  = UsbRescueMediumObserved { evidence: EvidenceRef, observed_at: EpochMs }
  | NetbootRescueMediumDeclared

// WHETHER A RESCUE MEDIUM HAS EVER BEEN RUN IS THE ARM IT IS, NOT A BOOL. Two predicates used to answer
// this -- `rescue_medium_is_executed` and `recovery_host_is_reachable` -- and the second called the first,
// so the same decision was made in two places that could disagree. Both are gone; the two sites that need
// the answer (the single constructor for RescueHostReachable, and the refusal the standing reads) match
// RescueHostMedium and RescueHostReachability themselves. `NetbootRescueMediumDeclared` remains a name
// with no evidence field to read, which is the whole enforcement: a declared provider has nothing to
// satisfy a reachability with.

// The driver stack the observed rescue host loads, named rather than "modules present" because each
// absence breaks the channel differently: ipmi_ssif binds the system interface (the host-to-BMC I2C
// bridge -- without it there is no in-band link at all), ipmi_msghandler is the message router,
// ipmi_devintf is what creates /dev/ipmi*, acpi_ipmi carries the ACPI-side IPMI description the
// bridge is declared through. An SSH session to a machine missing any of them reaches a Linux box
// that cannot talk to its own BMC, which is the same dead end as no session.
data recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName> = [
  "ipmi_ssif",
  "ipmi_devintf",
  "ipmi_msghandler",
  "acpi_ipmi",
]

data recovery_ipmi_device_node: NonEmptyStr = "/dev/ipmi0"

type InBandIpmiPresence {
  device_node: NonEmptyStr
  kernel_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName>
  evidence: EvidenceRef
}

fn in_band_ipmi_presence_is_complete(presence: InBandIpmiPresence) -> Bool {
  (presence.device_node as String) == (recovery_ipmi_device_node as String)
    && recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules |> all(m => presence.kernel_modules |> any(h => h == m))
}

fn missing_recovery_kernel_modules(presence: InBandIpmiPresence) -> List<LinuxKernelModuleName> {
  recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules |> filter(m => presence.kernel_modules |> any(h => h == m) == false)
}

// THE HONEST BOUNDARY, AND WHERE "PHYSICAL-ONLY" IS STATED. With no rescue OS we can reach there is
// no route in this module, no credential at the end of it, and no pretending otherwise. The cause is
// NAMED rather than a generic refusal because the consumer is gunbc.machine_intake.arrival_converge:
// its AccessDiscover arm has to tell an operator "insert the rescue medium / netboot this board" from
// "the rescue host is up but has no in-band IPMI -- this unit needs different hands" from "the only
// provider we model is a declared one".
type RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS
  = NoRescueHostAddressResponded { address: Ipv4Address, ssh_port: Int }
  | RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: Ipv4Address, missing_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName> }
  | RescueMediumNotExecuted { medium: RescueHostMedium }

type RescueHostReachability
  = RescueHostReachable {
      address: Ipv4Address
      ssh_port: Int
      ipmi: InBandIpmiPresence
      medium: RescueHostMedium
      evidence: EvidenceRef
      observed_at: EpochMs
    }
  | RescueHostUnreachable { cause: RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS }

fn recovery_host_refusal(reachability: RescueHostReachability) -> RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS? {
  match reachability {
    RescueHostReachable { address: _, ssh_port: _, ipmi: _, medium: medium, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } =>
      // The constructor below refuses a declared medium, so a RescueHostReachable carrying one is not
      // buildable; this arm is the honest complement rather than a second opinion.
      match medium {
        UsbRescueMediumObserved { evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => none
        NetbootRescueMediumDeclared => Present { value: RescueMediumNotExecuted { medium: medium } }
      }
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: c } => Present { value: c }
  }
}

// THE ONLY WAY A REACHABLE RESCUE HOST ENTERS THE MODEL. A rescue host that is "reachable" because a
// caller typed an address is the assertion this whole step exists to refuse, so nothing constructs
// RescueHostReachable except here -- and here the constructor checks its own inputs: the medium must
// be an executed one, the port must be a port, and the driver stack must actually be the one claimed.
// The executed check happens twice by design (here, and again in recovery_host_refusal) because the
// second is the one the standing reads, and a reader of the standing must not have to know how the
// value was built.
fn rescue_host_reachable(
  address: Ipv4Address,
  ssh_port: Int,
  ipmi: InBandIpmiPresence,
  medium: RescueHostMedium,
  evidence: EvidenceRef,
  observed_at: EpochMs,
) -> RescueHostReachability {
  if match medium {
    UsbRescueMediumObserved { evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
    NetbootRescueMediumDeclared => true
  } {
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: RescueMediumNotExecuted { medium: medium } }
  } else if ssh_port < 1 || ssh_port > 65535 {
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: NoRescueHostAddressResponded { address: address, ssh_port: ssh_port } }
  } else if in_band_ipmi_presence_is_complete(presence: ipmi) == false {
    RescueHostUnreachable {
      cause: RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi {
        address: address,
        missing_modules: missing_recovery_kernel_modules(presence: ipmi),
      },
    }
  } else {
    RescueHostReachable {
      address: address,
      ssh_port: ssh_port,
      ipmi: ipmi,
      medium: medium,
      evidence: evidence,
      observed_at: observed_at,
    }
  }
}

// ── STEP 2: THE IN-BAND CHANNEL, OBSERVED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS ROUTE EXIST AT ALL: the SSIF system interface needs no credential. On the captured unit every
// call was made against /dev/ipmi0 with no -I lanplus and no user name or password on the command
// line, and it still read the Administrator account out of the controller. That is the whole reason a
// board with an unknown password is reachable: the host-to-BMC interface inside the chassis
// authenticates by PHYSICAL POSITION, not by a secret. So the fact is not a comment beside a
// function -- it is a type, and it is the type the route admits on.
type InBandAccessWithoutCredential
  = InBandChannelPasswordless {
      interface: IpmiCommandInterface
      argv_credential_words: List<String>
    }
  | InBandChannelNotObserved { detail: NonEmptyStr }

// PASSWORDLESS IS A CLAIM ABOUT WHAT WAS *NOT* ON THE COMMAND LINE, so it is made checkable rather than
// asserted: the transport must be one of the arms that reaches the controller from inside its own chassis
// (extdeps.bmc.ipmi's closed sum, refused at the parse if the captured name did not map to one), and the
// credential-bearing words must be empty. An empty list is not nothing -- it is the recorded fact that the
// argv was read and carried no -I, no -U, no -P. A caller who passed a credential has to fill the list, and
// then this is false and the channel admits nothing.
//
// The transport is a SUM, not a string, and there is no deny-list here. An earlier revision compared the
// interface against ""/lanplus/lan/open, which failed open (a typo, a casing change, a trailing space, or a
// transport nobody listed all counted as in-band) AND refused the wrong thing: `open` is ipmitool's OpenIPMI
// driver over /dev/ipmi0 -- exactly the in-band, passwordless interface the captured unit used. The
// classification now lives in one place, ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport, and a transport
// that authenticates by a secret can never be admitted however it is spelled (§5).
fn in_band_channel_is_passwordless(channel: InBandAccessWithoutCredential) -> Bool {
  match channel {
    InBandChannelPasswordless { interface: interface, argv_credential_words: words } =>
      ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: interface)
        && list_length(items: words) == 0
    InBandChannelNotObserved { detail: _ } => false
  }
}

// The captured channel, read from the bytes: /dev/ipmi0 was present with ipmi_ssif loaded, and no -I was
// passed, so ipmitool used its default transport -- `open`, the OpenIPMI kernel driver over /dev/ipmi0
// (upstream's DEFAULT_INTF_NO_ENV when the driver is built). Note what the capture does NOT say: `ipmi_ssif`
// is the kernel MODULE for the SMBus/IPMI-SSIF bus beneath that driver, not a name `ipmitool -I` accepts;
// naming the interface "ssif" would have been a transport that does not exist, and it would now be refused
// at the parse rather than modeled.
// The out-of-band transport, built by the same route through the parse, for the law that requires it to be
// refused. It IS an arm of the sum -- ipmitool does have it -- it simply is not an in-band host path.
fn lan_plus_recovery_interface() -> IpmiCommandInterface {
  match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "lanplus") {
    IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, interface: interface } => interface
    IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => DummyTestInterface
  }
}

fn in_band_recovery_interface() -> IpmiCommandInterface {
  match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "open") {
    IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, interface: interface } => interface
    IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => DummyTestInterface
  }
}

// The account read back BEFORE anything is touched, so the reset is known to be aimed at a controller
// that has an Administrator account to lose. What `ipmitool user list 1` printed on the captured unit
// was id 1 / root / ADMINISTRATOR; the type asks for the id and the privilege that were read, not for
// the pair to match a literal, because the precondition is "an ADMINISTRATOR account was read back at
// this id on this channel" and not "the id was 1".
type InBandAdministratorAccount {
  user_id: IpmiUserId
  channel: IpmiChannelNumber
  privilege_is_administrator: Bool
  evidence: EvidenceRef
}

fn in_band_administrator_account_reads_back(account: InBandAdministratorAccount) -> Bool {
  account.privilege_is_administrator && account.user_id >= 1
}

// The channel observation as one carrier: which unit, which rescue host read it, the device node and
// drivers, the passwordless fact, and the Administrator account that came back.
type InBandChannelObservation {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  rescue_host_address: Ipv4Address
  device_node: NonEmptyStr
  kernel_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName>
  channel: InBandAccessWithoutCredential
  administrator: InBandAdministratorAccount
  evidence: EvidenceRef
  observed_at: EpochMs
}

// The channel has to have been read FROM THE HOST THAT WAS DECLARED REACHABLE, on the device and
// drivers that host reported. A channel observation lifted from a different machine, or from before
// the rescue OS booted, is not a precondition of anything -- it is a story about some BMC.
fn in_band_channel_admits(
  observation: InBandChannelObservation,
  reachability: RescueHostReachability,
) -> Bool {
  match reachability {
    RescueHostReachable { address: a, ssh_port: _, ipmi: ipmi, medium: _, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } =>
      in_band_channel_is_passwordless(channel: observation.channel)
        && in_band_administrator_account_reads_back(account: observation.administrator)
        && observation.rescue_host_address == a
        && (observation.device_node as String) == (ipmi.device_node as String)
        && observation.kernel_modules |> all(m => ipmi.kernel_modules |> any(h => h == m))
        && ipmi.kernel_modules |> all(m => observation.kernel_modules |> any(h => h == m))
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// ── STEP 3: THE ACTUATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two writes, both privileged, both registered as rows in gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_census and
// both run through gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection:
//
//   WRITE 1 -- the in-band Set User Password that puts a temporary credential on the Administrator
//     account, which is what makes the authenticated Redfish session of Write 2 possible (the
//     in-band channel cannot READ the existing password, so a known one is written). Reversible: the
//     next reset clears it and rotation replaces it. EXECUTED BUT NOT BYTE-CAPTURED -- see
//     RecoveryWriteRecord, and the capture README's own statement of it.
//
//   WRITE 2 -- POST /redfish/v1/Managers/<id>/Actions/Manager.ResetToDefaults with
//     ResetToDefaultsType=ResetAll. DESTRUCTIVE by construction: ResetAll returns every account,
//     every password, the network configuration and the user table to factory state, on a board being
//     brought into the fleet rather than serviced. Operator approval, and it owes a readback.
//
// WHY NOT PATCH THE PASSWORD VIA REDFISH INSTEAD: because that is the write the firmware refuses
// (PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy), and because even where it were allowed it would be a
// credential write standing in for the reset the unit needs -- the board is arriving from a prior
// life, and gunbc.machine_intake.prior_life_boundary is the module that prices leaving one.
type RedfishResetToDefaultsType = ResetAll | ResetNetwork | ResetRestore

// ResetRestore keeps the user table, which is precisely the table holding the unknown password;
// ResetNetwork likewise. Only ResetAll can produce a credential we hold. Spelled as a judgement on
// the arm rather than an if inside the actuator, so a future reader cannot reach for the gentler arm
// as a default and get a "recovery" that changed nothing an attacker would care about.
fn reset_to_defaults_clears_unknown_credential(kind: RedfishResetToDefaultsType) -> Bool {
  match kind {
    ResetAll => true
    ResetNetwork => false
    ResetRestore => false
  }
}

type RecoveryActuationKind
  = InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword
  | RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: RedfishResetToDefaultsType }

fn recovery_actuation_is_destructive(kind: RecoveryActuationKind) -> Bool {
  match kind {
    InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword => false
    RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: r } => reset_to_defaults_clears_unknown_credential(kind: r)
  }
}

fn recovery_reset_actuation() -> RecoveryActuationKind {
  RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: ResetAll }
}

// KIND AND ARM IDENTITY ARE THE CORPUS'S STRUCTURAL EQUALITY, NOT A HAND-WRITTEN TABLE. The two
// matchers this replaces enumerated every pair of variants -- 3x3 for the reset arm alone -- so each new
// RedfishResetToDefaultsType variant made them quadratically larger, and a missed pair silently read as
// "different" or "same" with nothing complaining (§2: model a concept once and derive every use). The
// canonical surface compares the values, which is the question actually being asked.
fn recovery_actuation_kinds_are_same(a: RecoveryActuationKind, b: RecoveryActuationKind) -> Bool {
  a == b
}

// The write record names which write it records, so the record matches the kind being asked about by
// VALUE and not by a table of arms.
fn recovery_write_record_names_kind(record: RecoveryWriteRecord, kind: RecoveryActuationKind) -> Bool {
  match record {
    ExecutedWithCapturedExchange { kind: k, request: _, response: _, observed_at: _ } => recovery_actuation_kinds_are_same(a: k, b: kind)
    ExecutedWithoutByteCapture { kind: k, observed_exit: _, detail: _ } => recovery_actuation_kinds_are_same(a: k, b: kind)
    NotExecuted { kind: _ } => false
  }
}

// WHAT THE WRITES LOOK LIKE IN THE LEDGER. Each is a gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_census row
// (recovery_in_band_set_user_password_effect, recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults_effect) in the standing
// scope selection assigns; the carrier below is the outcome of running the selection over those rows.
// It is a carrier and not a boolean because DESIGN §4d: a write is authorized, or it is refused with
// the refusal the selection actually gave. A destructive arm that arrives without its operator
// approval is REFUSED here even if some row said otherwise -- the approval is not decoration.
// THE ADMITTED ARM IS SPLIT BY KIND, SO THE ILLEGAL CARRIER IS UNWRITABLE (§5: correctness by
// construction, not validation). The carrier used to hold `kind` plus two Bools, `destructive` and
// `operator_approval_required`, that restated what the kind already says -- which made it writable to hold
// a ResetAll marked non-destructive, and `recovery_write_is_admissible` existed to catch that after the
// fact. Now the destructive arm CANNOT be built without carrying the operator-approval declaration it
// stands under, and the password arm has nowhere to put a destructive flag at all. Both Bools and the
// validator that patched them are gone: there is no longer a state for it to reject.
type RecoveryWriteAuthorization
  = RecoveryWritePasswordAdmitted { authorized_by: DeclarationRef }
  | RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted {
      reset_type: RedfishResetToDefaultsType
      authorized_by: DeclarationRef
      operator_ruling: StandingDestructiveAuthorization
    }
  | RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn recovery_authorization_is_admitted(authorization: RecoveryWriteAuthorization) -> Bool {
  match authorization {
    RecoveryWritePasswordAdmitted { authorized_by: _ } => true
    RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted { reset_type: _, authorized_by: _, operator_ruling: _ } => true
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// The two admitted arms carry the two census sites, so "who admitted this" is answered by the arm rather
// than by a field a caller could set to anything.
// WHETHER A WRITE RECORD PROVES ANYTHING AT ALL. Both privileged writes of this sequence were executed
// and NEITHER was byte-captured: the temporary password was wiped by the reset and cannot be reproduced,
// and the ResetToDefaults request/response bodies were observed in session only. That is what
// ExecutedWithoutByteCapture means, and the route's success condition does NOT rest on it -- it rests on
// the post-reset readback below. A write record can therefore prove an ATTEMPT and nothing more.
type RecoveryWriteRecord
  = ExecutedWithCapturedExchange {
      kind: RecoveryActuationKind
      request: EvidenceRef
      response: EvidenceRef
      observed_at: EpochMs
    }
  | ExecutedWithoutByteCapture { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, observed_exit: Int, detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | NotExecuted { kind: RecoveryActuationKind }

fn recovery_write_was_attempted(record: RecoveryWriteRecord, kind: RecoveryActuationKind) -> Bool {
  recovery_write_record_names_kind(record: record, kind: kind)
}

fn recovery_write_grounds_success(record: RecoveryWriteRecord) -> Bool {
  match record {
    ExecutedWithCapturedExchange { kind: _, request: _, response: _, observed_at: _ } => true
    ExecutedWithoutByteCapture { kind: _, observed_exit: _, detail: _ } => false
    NotExecuted { kind: _ } => false
  }
}

fn recovery_authorization_issued_by(authorization: RecoveryWriteAuthorization, gate: DeclarationRef) -> Bool {
  match authorization {
    RecoveryWritePasswordAdmitted { authorized_by: issuer } => declaration_ref_eq(a: issuer, b: gate)
    RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted { reset_type: _, authorized_by: issuer, operator_ruling: _ } =>
      declaration_ref_eq(a: issuer, b: gate)
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE ADMISSION GATES THEMSELVES: WHICH DECLARATIONS THE CENSUS ROWS ARE FILED AGAINST.
//
// The two recovery write rows in gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_census carry `site` pointing at the two
// functions below. That is the whole reason those rows exist: a census row whose decl_name names nothing is
// a classification of a write that is not made anywhere (DESIGN §3 -- "a stale symbol is decidable from the
// Node tree"; here it would be worse than stale, it was never there). A row must point at the code that
// DECIDES the write, so ledger and gate cannot drift: change the gate and the row is on its face describing
// something that no longer admits.
fn recovery_in_band_password_write_site() -> DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "admit_recovery_in_band_password_write")
}

fn recovery_manager_reset_site() -> DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults")
}

fn recovery_census_row_at(site_ref: DeclarationRef) -> PrivilegedEffectSite? {
  filter(privileged_effect_census, row => declaration_ref_eq(a: row.site, b: site_ref)) |> first()
}

// WHAT THE LEDGER HAS TO SAY ABOUT A RECOVERY WRITE. `site_verdict` is the census's own judgment: it asks
// gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection what pattern the effect's characteristics force, then joins
// that against what the row claims is realized. Nothing here is a boolean the route carries with it. Two
// questions are asked, at two strengths, because the two writes are not alike:
//
//   * `recovery_ledger_conforms` -- a row exists, its selection is a pattern (not a refusal, not "needs
//     evidence", not "not decidable"), and the row's `realized` agrees with what was selected. This is the
//     bar for the reversible temporary password; demanding operator approval there would have the census
//     claim a destructive standing for a write the next rotation undoes.
//   * `recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval` -- on top of that, the selected pattern IS
//     OperatorApprovedCapability and the row realizes an approval capability. Only the reset is held to
//     this, and what makes it a gate rather than a redundancy is that it reads the pattern the SELECTION
//     produced from the effect's characteristics: soften the reset's `reversibility` and selection moves to
//     a revocable grant, so the gate closes. A gate that merely restated the row's own field would be
//     decoration.
fn recovery_ledger_conforms(site_ref: DeclarationRef) -> Bool {
  match recovery_census_row_at(site_ref: site_ref) {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: row } =>
      match site_verdict(row: row) {
        SiteVerdict { site: _, selection: selection, conformance: conformance } =>
          match selection {
            PatternSelected { pattern: _, receipt: _ } =>
              match conformance {
                Conforms => true
                Diverges => false
                DivergesWithReason { reason: _ } => false
                SiteNotDecidable { standing: _ } => false
              }
            ManualStepRequired { step: _, receipt: _ } => false
            PatternSelectionRefused { defects: _ } => false
            PatternSelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: _ } => false
            PatternSelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: _ } => false
            NoAdmissiblePattern { causes: _, scope: _ } => false
          }
      }
  }
}

fn recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval(site_ref: DeclarationRef) -> Bool {
  match recovery_census_row_at(site_ref: site_ref) {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: row } =>
      match site_verdict(row: row) {
        SiteVerdict { site: _, selection: selection, conformance: conformance } =>
          match selection {
            PatternSelected { pattern: OperatorApprovedCapability, receipt: _ } =>
              match conformance {
                Conforms => match row.realized {
                  RealizedApprovalCapability => true
                  _ => false
                }
                Diverges => false
                DivergesWithReason { reason: _ } => false
                SiteNotDecidable { standing: _ } => false
              }
            ManualStepRequired { step: _, receipt: _ } => false
            PatternSelectionRefused { defects: _ } => false
            PatternSelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: _ } => false
            PatternSelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: _ } => false
            NoAdmissiblePattern { causes: _, scope: _ } => false
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE GATE ON THE IN-BAND PASSWORD WRITE. Admits only against its own census row, whose selection the
// selection module derives from the effect's own characteristics, and refuses unless the channel the route
// claims really was passwordless -- `in_band_channel_is_passwordless` is re-checked on the carried channel
// rather than trusted from the outcome. That re-check is the point: a temporary Administrator password is
// being written into the controller's user table by a caller asserting that no credential was involved, and
// the carrier for that assertion is the argv the channel observation carries.
fn admit_recovery_in_band_password_write(route: RecoveryInBandRoute) -> RecoveryWriteAuthorization {
  let kind = recovery_in_band_password_actuation()
  if recovery_ledger_conforms(site_ref: recovery_in_band_password_write_site()) == false {
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: kind, cause: "the census verdict at this site does not select the operator-approved capability the effect's classification forces" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if in_band_channel_is_passwordless(channel: route.channel.admits) == false {
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: kind, cause: "the in-band channel is not admitted as passwordless, so this is not the write the census row classifies" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    RecoveryWritePasswordAdmitted { authorized_by: recovery_in_band_password_write_site() }
  }
}

// THE GATE ON THE DESTRUCTIVE RESET -- the only place a ResetAll may be decided. Each condition is a
// different mistake, so each has its own cause:
//   * the ledger admits it: the census row exists and its selection is the operator-approved capability
//     (irreversibility forces that -- a grant that could be revoked cannot stand behind what no reapply
//     undoes), and the row's `realized` agrees rather than merely sitting beside it;
//   * the run acts under the named recovery ruling, matched on BOTH fields so a destructive arm cannot
//     borrow some other standing's blanket approval, and the ruling's interlock is actually rostered --
//     `ruling_discharge_interlock_defect` refuses a pending interlock, which is the case today, so this
//     gate is closed until the hold is rostered rather than pretending to be open;
//   * the arm is the one that clears the user table (an arm that keeps the user table is a destructive
//     actuation that does not solve the problem justifying it);
//   * this firmware release was measured refusing the published password, matched as the coproduct it is;
//   * the unit's durable hold is held, and held for THIS unit: a ResetAll returns every account, every
//     password and the network configuration of one chassis to factory state, so it may not be actuated on
//     a unit somebody else is driving, nor on a unit whose key merely shares a prefix with the subject.
fn admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  under_ruling: StandingDestructiveAuthorization,
  unit_hold: UnitHoldAcquisition?,
) -> RecoveryWriteAuthorization {
  let kind = recovery_reset_actuation()
  if recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval(site_ref: recovery_manager_reset_site()) == false {
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: kind, cause: "the reset is not admitted by the census verdict: no operator-approved capability realized at this site" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if match ruling_discharge_interlock_defect(site_ref: recovery_manager_reset_site(), ruling: under_ruling) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  } {
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: kind, cause: "the ruling's interlock is not rostered against this site, so no standing discharges the reset's irreversibility" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if route.reset.reset_type != ResetAll {
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: kind, cause: "the arm is not ResetAll, so it keeps the user table holding the unknown password" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if recovery_reset_is_authorized_by_measurement(policy: route.password_write_policy) == false {
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: kind, cause: "this firmware release has no measured refusal, so a reset is not warranted" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if recovery_hold_admits_this_unit(unit_key: route.subject.qualification.unit_key, hold: unit_hold) == false {
    RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: kind, cause: "the unit hold is not held for this unit, so the chassis is not this run's to wipe" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    // the admitted arm carries the ruling it stands under and the arm it cleared, so a later reader can
    // see WHICH approval purchased WHICH destructive actuation
    RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted {
      reset_type: route.reset.reset_type,
      authorized_by: recovery_manager_reset_site(),
      operator_ruling: under_ruling,
    }
  }
}

// THE HOLD, AS OBSERVED. Absent means the run never took the hold, which is a refusal and not a default;
// a refused acquisition is a refusal carrying the store's own words about who holds the unit; and the proof
// that IS held must name the SAME unit as the recovery subject -- compared as whole strings, not as prefixes
// or by eye. A recovery that wipes a chassis while a different run holds its maintenance hold is the failure
// this exists to make unrepresentable. The proof itself cannot be fabricated here: UnitHoldProof is minted
// only by the hold module's admit_callers list, so this predicate can be exercised in its refusals and the
// admitting arm is gated by construction rather than by a flag.
fn recovery_hold_admits_this_unit(unit_key: UnitKey, hold: UnitHoldAcquisition?) -> Bool {
  match hold {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: acquisition } =>
      match acquisition {
        UnitHoldRefused { reason: _ } => false
        UnitHeld { proof: proof } =>
          match proof.subject {
            AdmittedUnitHoldSubject { unit: unit } => (unit as String) == (unit_key as String)
          }
      }
  }
}

// AN ADMISSION IS ONLY GOOD IF THE GATE THAT OWNS THE WRITE ISSUED IT, and the arm it arrived in is what
// says so: the password arm can only name the password site's shape, and the reset arm does not exist
// unless it carries an operator ruling. converge checks the issuer against the census site that classifies
// the write, so an arm minted for one gate cannot purchase the other's effect.

// "IT REFUSED" IS NOT THE SUCCESS SIGNAL. A 401 means the credential we sent is not the credential
// the controller holds -- including the case where the reset did nothing at all. What says the reset
// happened is HTTP 403 naming Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired: the published factory credential
// AUTHENTICATED (so it is the active password again) and the service then refused the request until
// that password is changed. Both shapes are committed bytes:
//   artifacts/bmc/srv13-first-contact-2026-10-07/bmc-3.22.00-factory-login-403.json
//   artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/04_post_reset_readback.json
// The verdict comes from the Redfish message predicate rather than a literal equality here, because
// the registry version travels with the service (Base.1.16.0 on these units today) and a hard
// equality would stop matching silently the day a firmware bumps it.
type RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback
  = FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
      message_id: NonEmptyStr
      evidence: EvidenceRef
      observed_at: EpochMs
    }
  | CredentialNotAccepted { evidence: EvidenceRef, observed_at: EpochMs }
  | ReadbackInconclusive { detail: NonEmptyStr }

fn recovery_readback_proves_reset(readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback) -> Bool {
  match readback {
    FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange { message_id, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } =>
      redfish_message_id_is_password_change_required(id: message_id as String)
    CredentialNotAccepted { evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
    ReadbackInconclusive { detail: _ } => false
  }
}

// The arms as the message decides them, so they cannot be chosen by taste: a body that does not name
// the message is inconclusive, and a readback is never built from an operator's account of the
// status line alone.
fn recovery_readback_of(body: String, evidence: EvidenceRef, observed_at: EpochMs) -> RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback {
  match redfish_password_change_verdict(body: body) {
    RedfishPasswordChangeRequired =>
      FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
        message_id: "Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired",
        evidence: evidence,
        observed_at: observed_at,
      }
    RedfishPasswordChangeNotIndicated =>
      ReadbackInconclusive { detail: "the post-reset response named no PasswordChangeRequired message" }
  }
}

// ── THE ROUTE RECORD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// CARRIED FIELDS, AND WHY. The rescue host address and the in-band device node are this route's own
// facts -- nothing else in the intake records that a rescue OS sat at that address, on that node --
// so they live here. The CONTROLLER is carried as the observed BmcAccessObservation rather than as an
// endpoint string, so the recovery is checked against the SAME endpoint the control-route join checks;
// a bare endpoint would be a second, unchecked copy of a fact whose owner is elsewhere, which is the
// reflash-staleness defect cut 2 just deleted. The write policy rides along with its release identity
// so the refusal to write the published password is always a per-release fact read off the catalog,
// and never a claim attached to the recovery.
type RecoveryInBandRoute {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  rescue_host: RescueHostReachability
  in_band: InBandChannelObservation
  controller: BmcAccessObservation
  password_write_policy: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy
  operator_approval: DeclarationRef
  reset_readback: DeclarationRef
}

// ── THE TERMINAL STANDING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ONE SEALED CARRIER, AND THE HANDOFF IS ITS ONLY SUCCESS ARM. RecoveryHandedToOnboarding carries no
// credential and no binding: it carries the handoff -- the observed controller the existing
// factory-login-then-rotate path is pointed at -- and the readback that justifies believing the
// factory credential is active. From there gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_secure produces the
// BmcSecureStanding and the control-route join mints the binding; this module does neither. Every
// other arm is a named reason the route stopped, and each is a DIFFERENT next action for whoever is
// standing at the machine.
type InBandRecoveryOutcome
  = RecoveryHandedToOnboarding {
      controller: BmcAccessObservation
      readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback
      writes: List<RecoveryWriteRecord>
    }
  | RecoveryHostUnreachable { cause: RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS }
  | RecoveryChannelUnobserved { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | RecoveryFirmwarePolicyNotMeasured { firmware: BmcFirmwareVersion }
  | RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: RecoveryActuationKind }
  | RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback }

type InBandRecoveryStanding sole_constructor {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute
  outcome: InBandRecoveryOutcome
}

fn recovery_standing_handed_off(standing: InBandRecoveryStanding) -> Bool {
  match standing.outcome {
    RecoveryHandedToOnboarding { controller: _, readback: r, writes: _ } => recovery_readback_proves_reset(readback: r)
    RecoveryHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => false
    RecoveryChannelUnobserved { detail: _ } => false
    RecoveryFirmwarePolicyNotMeasured { firmware: _ } => false
    RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: _ } => false
    RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: _ } => false
  }
}

// ── THE CONVERGENCE STEPS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ORDER IS NOT A COMMENT. Each step's precondition names the step before it, and a step whose
// precondition was not read back in this run refuses. A recovery is exactly the sequence an operator
// might be tempted to enter in the middle -- a reset without a channel observation, or a handoff
// without a readback -- and the fold, not a prose rule, is what refuses it.
data recovery_reachable_host_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_reachable_host_step",
)

data recovery_channel_observation_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_channel_observation_step",
)

data recovery_in_band_password_write_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_in_band_password_write_step",
)

data recovery_reset_to_defaults_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_reset_to_defaults_step",
)

data recovery_factory_credential_readback_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_factory_credential_readback_step",
)

data recovery_onboard_handoff_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_onboard_handoff_step",
)

data recovery_operator_approval_declaration: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_operator_approval_declaration",
)

fn recovery_convergence_steps() -> List<DeclarationRef> {
  [
    recovery_reachable_host_step,
    recovery_channel_observation_step,
    recovery_in_band_password_write_step,
    recovery_reset_to_defaults_step,
    recovery_factory_credential_readback_step,
    recovery_onboard_handoff_step,
  ]
}

// What the handoff requires to have been read back first. The readback step is required as a STEP and
// its content is required again in the standing: one makes the sequence honest, the other makes the
// standing unprovable from an operator's word that the reset "looked like it worked".
fn recovery_handoff_requires() -> List<DeclarationRef> {
  [
    recovery_reachable_host_step,
    recovery_channel_observation_step,
    recovery_reset_to_defaults_step,
    recovery_factory_credential_readback_step,
  ]
}

// THE ONE CONSTRUCTOR, AND WHAT IT REFUSES. Every step is checked here, so there is no path to
// RecoveryHandedToOnboarding that skips the readback, ignores a NotMeasured firmware, enters on a
// declared-but-unexecuted medium, or accepts a ResetNetwork/ResetRestore as a recovery. The writes are
// checked for having been ATTEMPTED against the arm the route names (their own byte capture is not the
// success condition, per RecoveryWriteRecord); the readback is checked for HAVING PROVEN the factory
// credential is active.
fn converge_in_band_recovery(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  in_band_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
  reset_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
  readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback,
  in_band_admission: RecoveryWriteAuthorization,
  reset_admission: RecoveryWriteAuthorization,
) -> InBandRecoveryStanding {
  match recovery_host_refusal(reachability: route.rescue_host) {
    Present { value: c } =>
      InBandRecoveryStanding { subject: route.subject, route: route, outcome: RecoveryHostUnreachable { cause: c } }
    Absent =>
      if in_band_channel_admits(observation: route.in_band, reachability: route.rescue_host) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryChannelUnobserved {
            detail: "the in-band channel was not read from the rescue host declared reachable, was not passwordless, or read no ADMINISTRATOR account",
          },
        }
      } else if recovery_reset_is_authorized_by_measurement(policy: route.password_write_policy) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryFirmwarePolicyNotMeasured { firmware: recovery_policy_firmware(policy: route.password_write_policy) },
        }
      } else if recovery_authorization_issued_by(authorization: in_band_admission, gate: recovery_in_band_password_write_site()) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
            kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
            cause: "the password write was not admitted by the gate its census row is filed at" as NonEmptyStr,
          },
        }
      } else if recovery_authorization_issued_by(authorization: reset_admission, gate: recovery_manager_reset_site()) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
            kind: recovery_reset_actuation(),
            cause: "the reset was not admitted by the gate its census row is filed at" as NonEmptyStr,
          },
        }
      } else if recovery_authorization_is_admitted(authorization: in_band_admission) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: match in_band_admission {
            RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: k, cause: cause } => RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: k, cause: cause }
            RecoveryWritePasswordAdmitted { authorized_by: _ } => RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(), cause: "unreachable: admitted arm" as NonEmptyStr }
            RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted { reset_type: _, authorized_by: _, operator_ruling: _ } => RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(), cause: "the password write arrived in the reset arm" as NonEmptyStr }
          },
        }
      } else if recovery_authorization_is_admitted(authorization: reset_admission) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: match reset_admission {
            RecoveryWriteRefused { kind: k, cause: cause } => RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: k, cause: cause }
            RecoveryWritePasswordAdmitted { authorized_by: _ } => RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: recovery_reset_actuation(), cause: "the reset arrived in the password arm, which carries no ruling" as NonEmptyStr }
            RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted { reset_type: _, authorized_by: _, operator_ruling: _ } => RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: recovery_reset_actuation(), cause: "unreachable: admitted arm" as NonEmptyStr }
          },
        }
      } else if recovery_write_was_attempted(record: in_band_write, kind: InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword },
        }
      } else if recovery_write_was_attempted(record: reset_write, kind: recovery_reset_actuation()) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: recovery_reset_actuation() },
        }
      } else if recovery_readback_proves_reset(readback: readback) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: readback },
        }
      } else {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryHandedToOnboarding {
            controller: route.controller,
            readback: readback,
            writes: [in_band_write, reset_write],
          },
        }
      }
  }
}

fn recovery_policy_firmware(policy: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy) -> BmcFirmwareVersion {
  match policy {
    FirmwareRefusesPublishedPassword { firmware: f, completion_code: _, capture: _ } => f
    NotMeasured { firmware: f } => f
  }
}

// THE ONE MEASUREMENT THE RESET RESTS ON, MATCHED AS THE COPRODUCT IT IS. There is deliberately no
// boolean here: a Bool over this coproduct would collapse "this release was measured refusing" and
// "nobody ever looked" into one bit the reader cannot interrogate, and the two demand opposite
// actions -- one authorizes a factory reset, the other refuses the route. Matching the arm is the
// check. Nor does this module re-check the completion code: the row is keyed by the release, so the
// arm the catalog returns IS the measurement, and its code is asserted where the code lives
// (extdeps.bmc.ipmi) and where the row is filed (extdeps.boards.asrock_rack). Re-asserting it here
// would be a second copy of one fact, free to drift from the row it claims to vouch for.
fn recovery_reset_is_authorized_by_measurement(policy: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy) -> Bool {
  match policy {
    FirmwareRefusesPublishedPassword { firmware: _, completion_code: _, capture: _ } => true
    NotMeasured { firmware: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE HANDOFF, AS THE QUESTION IT ASKS THE EXISTING PATH. Not a credential, not a secret, not a
// binding: the request to run factory-login-then-rotate against THIS observed controller. The
// consumers are gunbc.tools.bmc_onboard's plan (FactoryCredentialActive -> BmcStoreAndRotate) and
// gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_secure; neither is edited here, and nothing here can mint their standing.
type RecoveryOnboardHandoff {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  controller: BmcAccessObservation
  secured: BmcSecureStanding
}

fn recovery_handoff_targets_same_controller(handoff: RecoveryOnboardHandoff, route: RecoveryInBandRoute) -> Bool {
  handoff.controller == route.controller && handoff.subject == route.subject
}

// THE ROUTE'S REFUSAL, AS ONE CAUSE THE CONSUMER CAN BRANCH ON -- carried on the AccessDiscover arm
// in gunbc.machine_intake.arrival_converge. RecoveryRouteUnavailable is deliberately NOT
// AccessObserveRefusal's ControllerUnreachable: that one says the controller did not answer
// out-of-band, and the whole point of this module is that the controller may be answering perfectly
// well while being unreachable TO US. Collapsing the two would make a recoverable unit look identical
// to a dead one, which is how a board that needs a USB stick gets quarantined.
type RecoveryRouteUnavailable
  = NoReachableRescueHost { cause: RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS }
  | RecoveryFirmwarePolicyUnknown { firmware: BmcFirmwareVersion }
  | RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { detail: NonEmptyStr }

fn recovery_route_is_available(standing: InBandRecoveryStanding) -> Bool {
  recovery_standing_handed_off(standing: standing)
}

// The next PHYSICAL action, spelled out of the named cause. Two of these need nobody at the machine
// and one does; the consumer tells them apart only because the cause is named rather than collapsed
// into one "unavailable".
fn recovery_route_next_action(unavailable: RecoveryRouteUnavailable) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match unavailable {
    NoReachableRescueHost { cause: c } =>
      match c {
        NoRescueHostAddressResponded { address: _, ssh_port: _ } =>
          "bring up the rescue OS on the board (insert the prepared rescue medium, or netboot it once a netboot provider is executed) -- nothing is reachable at the rescue address" as NonEmptyStr
        RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: _, missing_modules: _ } =>
          "the rescue host is up but has no in-band IPMI: load the missing ipmi modules; without /dev/ipmi0 there is no route to the controller from inside the chassis" as NonEmptyStr
        RescueMediumNotExecuted { medium: _ } =>
          "the only rescue provider modelled for this unit is declared and has never been executed; use the executed medium or treat the unit as physical-only" as NonEmptyStr
      }
    RecoveryFirmwarePolicyUnknown { firmware: _ } =>
      "this firmware release has never been measured for the published-password write policy: read it before spending a destructive reset" as NonEmptyStr
    RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { detail: _ } =>
      "re-read the controller after the reset: the route is only believed once the login answers with the password-change refusal" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn recovery_route_unavailability(standing: InBandRecoveryStanding) -> RecoveryRouteUnavailable? {
  match standing.outcome {
    RecoveryHandedToOnboarding { controller: _, readback: _, writes: _ } => none
    RecoveryHostUnreachable { cause: c } => Present { value: NoReachableRescueHost { cause: c } }
    RecoveryChannelUnobserved { detail: d } => Present { value: RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { detail: d } }
    RecoveryFirmwarePolicyNotMeasured { firmware: f } => Present { value: RecoveryFirmwarePolicyUnknown { firmware: f } }
    RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: _ } =>
      Present { value: RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { detail: "a privileged write of the reset sequence was not attempted against the arm the route names" } }
    // A write the ledger does not admit leaves the unit locked AND the route blocked, so it is a
    // named requirement rather than a silent stop: the reader has to be told which write was refused,
    // because "go find a rescue USB" would be the wrong next action for it.
    RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: _, cause: cause } =>
      Present { value: RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { detail: cause } }
    RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: _ } =>
      Present { value: RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { detail: "the post-reset readback named no PasswordChangeRequired message" } }
  }
}

// ── COMMITTED EVIDENCE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// CITED BY SHA256, AND ONLY WHAT WAS CAPTURED. These digests are the entries of
// artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/SHA256SUMS, so a standing that claims a
// capture is checked against the file bytes and not against a sentence about them. The capture is
// committed at 518342bf41d on session/royal-seal-46 (the run was executed there); until those paths
// land on main, a digest below is verifiable against THAT commit and no other -- check it there before
// treating a standing as evidenced, and treat it as unevidenced if the commit is unreachable.
//
//   02_inband_channel.txt -- the rescue OS (Linux ubuntu-server 6.8.0-139-generic aarch64,
//     openssh-server: the HOST OS and not the BMC), /dev/ipmi0 crw------- 235,0, the four ipmi
//     modules, `mc info` (Firmware Revision 3.22, ASRock Rack), `channel 1 info`, `user list 1`
//     (id 1 root ADMINISTRATOR), `lan print 1`, and the two refused `user set password` calls.
//   04_post_reset_readback.json -- the post-reset login with the published factory credential,
//     answered HTTP 403 with Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired.
//
// NOT cited, because it was not captured: the in-band temporary-password write, and the ResetToDefaults
// request/response bodies. The capture README records both as in-session-observed; the temporary
// password was wiped by the reset and cannot be reproduced. That is what
// RecoveryWriteRecord::ExecutedWithoutByteCapture is for, and why the standing's success arm leans on
// the readback instead.
data recovery_inband_channel_capture: Sha256Digest = Sha256Digest {
  hex: "8c1750d13a6c9832b459a61b8aadab246f4e74f138d85861e2758200c07202a4",
}

data recovery_post_reset_readback_capture: Sha256Digest = Sha256Digest {
  hex: "898fef2cf2d57fcca4564b11b3630c050a34d85804e32cfd827fc22fa6da6ce1",
}

data recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref: EvidenceRef = EvidenceRef {
  label: "artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/02_inband_channel.txt",
  digest: Sha256Hash(recovery_inband_channel_capture),
}

data recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref: EvidenceRef = EvidenceRef {
  label: "artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/04_post_reset_readback.json",
  digest: Sha256Hash(recovery_post_reset_readback_capture),
}

// ── DOMAIN LAWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each states a non-invertibility this module depends on, and each is built only through the mints --
// a reachable host is unreachable until the drivers are present and the medium executed, and a
// standing is handed off only once the readback names the right message. A fixture that hand-built a
// reachable host or a standing would be proving the test and not the module.

test fn a_rescue_host_without_the_ipmi_stack_is_unreachable_and_says_which_module_is_missing() -> Bool {
  let reached = rescue_host_reachable(
    address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    ssh_port: 22,
    ipmi: InBandIpmiPresence {
      device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
      kernel_modules: ["ipmi_devintf", "ipmi_msghandler", "acpi_ipmi"],
      evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    },
    medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
  )
  match reached {
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: a, missing_modules: m } } =>
      a == recovery_rescue_host_address()
        && list_length(items: m) == 1
        && m |> first() == Present { value: "ipmi_ssif" }
    _ => false
  }
}

test fn a_declared_netboot_provider_never_satisfies_the_precondition_and_is_not_the_same_refusal_as_no_host() -> Bool {
  let via_netboot = rescue_host_reachable(
    address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    ssh_port: 22,
    ipmi: recovery_complete_ipmi_presence(),
    medium: NetbootRescueMediumDeclared,
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
  )
  match recovery_host_refusal(reachability: via_netboot) {
    Present { value: RescueMediumNotExecuted { medium: NetbootRescueMediumDeclared } } =>
      // and it landed on the UNREACHABLE arm, matched as the coproduct rather than through a Bool that
      // would restate the same decision a second time
      match via_netboot {
        RescueHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => true
        RescueHostReachable { address: _, ssh_port: _, ipmi: _, medium: _, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
      }
    _ => false
  }
}

test fn a_channel_carried_in_from_another_host_admits_nothing() -> Bool {
  let reached = rescue_host_reachable(
    address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    ssh_port: 22,
    ipmi: recovery_complete_ipmi_presence(),
    medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
  )
  let observation = InBandChannelObservation {
    subject: recovery_test_subject(),
    rescue_host_address: recovery_controller_address(),
    device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
    kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
    channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { interface: in_band_recovery_interface(), argv_credential_words: [] },
    administrator: recovery_administrator_account(),
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
  }
  in_band_channel_admits(observation: observation, reachability: reached) == false
    // and a declared medium never reaches the reachable arm at all -- matched as the coproduct it is
    && match reached {
        RescueHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => true
        RescueHostReachable { address: _, ssh_port: _, ipmi: _, medium: _, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
      }
}

test fn a_channel_that_passed_a_credential_on_the_command_line_is_not_passwordless() -> Bool {
  in_band_channel_is_passwordless(
    channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { interface: in_band_recovery_interface(), argv_credential_words: ["-P", "0penBmc"] },
  ) == false
    && in_band_channel_is_passwordless(
      channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { interface: lan_plus_recovery_interface(), argv_credential_words: [] },
    ) == false
    && in_band_channel_is_passwordless(
      channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { interface: in_band_recovery_interface(), argv_credential_words: [] },
    )
}

// THE SUM IS CLOSED, AND IT REFUSES IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. The reviewer's ask, stated as a law: the
// interface the captured unit actually used -- `open`, the OpenIPMI driver over /dev/ipmi0 -- is ADMITTED
// (the earlier deny-list refused it, which would have blocked the only route that exists for this board),
// and a name that does not map to an arm is REFUSED rather than assumed in-band. Casing and a trailing
// space are not interfaces ipmitool would load -- its dispatch is strcmp against ipmi_intf_table -- so a
// capture that appears to name them was read wrong, and that refusal is worth having rather than
// silently inventing a transport in front of a destructive reset.
test fn the_interface_sum_admits_the_captured_transport_and_refuses_anything_unlisted() -> Bool {
  // admitted: the in-band arms, including the one this capture used
  (match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "open") {
    IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: w, interface: i } => w == "open" && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: i)
    IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => false
  })
    && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: BmcKernelDriver)
    && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: UsbTransport)
    // refused: out-of-band transports that authenticate by a secret, and the arms that are not a host path
    && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: LanTransport) == false
    && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: lan_plus_recovery_interface()) == false
    && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: DbusLocalService) == false
    && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: SerialTerminal) == false
    && ipmi_command_interface_is_in_band_host_transport(interface: DummyTestInterface) == false
    // refused at the PARSE: a transport nobody named, in any spelling
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "ssif") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: w } => w == "ssif" IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, interface: _ } => false }
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "LANPLUS") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => true IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, interface: _ } => false }
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "lanplus ") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => true IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, interface: _ } => false }
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => true IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, interface: _ } => false }
    // and an unparseable name can never be handed to the channel predicate as if it were in-band: the
    // carrier has no arm for it, so the refusal above is the only way the observation can land
    && in_band_channel_is_passwordless(
      channel: InBandChannelNotObserved { detail: "ipmitool reported an interface this model has no arm for" },
    ) == false
}

test fn a_reset_arm_that_keeps_the_user_table_is_never_a_recovery() -> Bool {
  reset_to_defaults_clears_unknown_credential(kind: ResetAll)
    && reset_to_defaults_clears_unknown_credential(kind: ResetNetwork) == false
    && reset_to_defaults_clears_unknown_credential(kind: ResetRestore) == false
    && recovery_actuation_is_destructive(kind: RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: ResetRestore }) == false
    // The arm that used to be writable -- a ResetAll carried as non-destructive, or without a ruling --
    // has no constructor any more, so this asserts what §5 asks for: the shape cannot be built. What the
    // admitted reset arm DOES carry is the ruling, and it is the named one, not any standing's blanket.
    && recovery_authorization_is_admitted(authorization: recovery_admitted_reset_write())
    && recovery_authorization_issued_by(authorization: recovery_admitted_reset_write(), gate: recovery_manager_reset_site())
    && recovery_authorization_issued_by(authorization: recovery_admitted_reset_write(), gate: recovery_in_band_password_write_site()) == false
    && match recovery_admitted_reset_write() {
        RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted { reset_type: rt, authorized_by: _, operator_ruling: r } =>
          rt == ResetAll && r.ruling_text == recovery_reset_operator_ruling.ruling_text
        _ => false
      }
}

test fn a_readback_that_names_a_different_message_does_not_prove_the_reset() -> Bool {
  recovery_readback_proves_reset(
    readback: FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
      message_id: "Base.1.16.0.NoResource",
      evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
    },
  ) == false
    && recovery_readback_proves_reset(
      readback: FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
        message_id: "Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired",
        evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
        observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
      },
    )
    && recovery_readback_proves_reset(
      readback: CredentialNotAccepted {
        evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
        observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
      },
    ) == false
    && recovery_readback_of(
      body: "{\"error\":{\"@Message.ExtendedInfo\":[{\"MessageId\":\"Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired\"}]}}",
      evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
    ) == recovery_factory_credential_readback()
}

test fn a_release_the_catalog_lacks_refuses_the_route_rather_than_asserting_recovery() -> Bool {
  let unmeasured = asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_for(firmware: recovery_unmeasured_firmware())
  let measured = recovery_measured_refusal_policy()
  // each release lands on the arm it belongs to, and only one of them warrants a reset
  (match unmeasured { NotMeasured { firmware: _ } => true FirmwareRefusesPublishedPassword { firmware: _, completion_code: _, capture: _ } => false })
  && (match measured { NotMeasured { firmware: _ } => false FirmwareRefusesPublishedPassword { firmware: _, completion_code: _, capture: _ } => true })
  && recovery_firmware_requires_reset(firmware: recovery_measured_firmware())
  && recovery_firmware_requires_reset(firmware: recovery_unmeasured_firmware()) == false
}

test fn the_standing_refuses_to_hand_off_without_the_readback() -> Bool {
  let route = recovery_test_route(policy: recovery_measured_refusal_policy())
  let without_readback = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: route,
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: ReadbackInconclusive { detail: "no body was saved" },
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  let with_readback = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: route,
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  recovery_standing_handed_off(standing: without_readback) == false
    && recovery_standing_handed_off(standing: with_readback)
    && match without_readback.outcome {
      RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: _ } => true
      _ => false
    }
    && recovery_route_unavailability(standing: without_readback)
      == Present { value: RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { detail: "the post-reset readback named no PasswordChangeRequired message" } }
}

test fn an_unattempted_reset_write_leaves_the_route_unverified_rather_than_recovered() -> Bool {
  let standing = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: recovery_test_route(policy: recovery_measured_refusal_policy()),
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: NotExecuted { kind: recovery_reset_actuation() },
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  match standing.outcome {
    RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: ResetAll } } =>
      recovery_standing_handed_off(standing: standing) == false
    _ => false
  }
}

test fn a_policy_that_was_never_measured_on_this_release_cannot_ground_a_destructive_reset() -> Bool {
  let standing = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: recovery_test_route(policy: NotMeasured { firmware: recovery_measured_firmware() }),
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  match standing.outcome {
    RecoveryFirmwarePolicyNotMeasured { firmware: f } =>
      f.version.major == 3 && f.version.minor == 22 && recovery_standing_handed_off(standing: standing) == false
    _ => false
  }
}

// ── FIXTURES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The capture's own numbers, through the mints only.

data recovery_capture_instant: EpochMs = 1760000000000

fn recovery_rescue_host_address() -> Ipv4Address {
  Ipv4Address { octet1: 192, octet2: 168, octet3: 1, octet4: 251 }
}

fn recovery_controller_address() -> Ipv4Address {
  Ipv4Address { octet1: 192, octet2: 168, octet3: 1, octet4: 250 }
}

// The release the capture's `mc info` reported (Firmware Revision 3.22), spelled through the
// catalog's own row rather than re-authored: the policy join is keyed by typed version, so a fixture
// with its own number would be testing a firmware that does not exist.
fn recovery_measured_firmware() -> BmcFirmwareVersion {
  asrock_altrad8ud_openbmc_3_22_00_published_factory_password_write_policy.firmware
}

fn recovery_unmeasured_firmware() -> BmcFirmwareVersion {
  BmcFirmwareVersion { family: OpenBmc, version: firmware_semantic_version(major: 3, minor: 6, patch: 0) }
}

fn recovery_test_release() -> BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity {
  BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity { family: OpenBmc, raw_version: "3.22", semantic_version: none }
}

fn recovery_usb_medium() -> RescueHostMedium {
  UsbRescueMediumObserved { evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref, observed_at: recovery_capture_instant }
}

fn recovery_complete_ipmi_presence() -> InBandIpmiPresence {
  InBandIpmiPresence {
    device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
    kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
  }
}

fn recovery_administrator_account() -> InBandAdministratorAccount {
  InBandAdministratorAccount {
    user_id: 1,
    channel: 1,
    privilege_is_administrator: true,
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
  }
}

fn recovery_test_subject() -> MachineIntakeSubject {
  MachineIntakeSubject {
    subject: QualificationSubject {
      unit_key: "bmc-recovery-fixture-unit",
      assembly_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "assembly-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
      firmware_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "firmware-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
    },
    attempt_id: "in-band-recovery-attempt-1",
  }
}

fn recovery_test_controller_observation() -> BmcAccessObservation {
  BmcAccessObservation {
    endpoint: BmcControllerEndpoint { host: "bmc-recovery-fixture-controller" },
    identity: BmcObservedIdentity {
      board: BoardFruNotRead { cause: BoardAreaFieldsUnreadable },
      chassis_part_number: none,
      firmware: recovery_test_release(),
    },
    discovered_surfaces: [],
    boot_control_routes: [],
    redfish_virtual_media_probe: none,
    successful_read_surfaces: [],
    successful_write_surfaces: [],
    raw_response: content_hash_of_value(value: "in-band-recovery-fixture-raw-response"),
    observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
  }
}

fn recovery_test_route(policy: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy) -> RecoveryInBandRoute {
  RecoveryInBandRoute {
    subject: recovery_test_subject(),
    rescue_host: rescue_host_reachable(
      address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
      ssh_port: 22,
      ipmi: recovery_complete_ipmi_presence(),
      medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
      evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
    ),
    in_band: InBandChannelObservation {
      subject: recovery_test_subject(),
      rescue_host_address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
      device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
      kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
      channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { interface: in_band_recovery_interface(), argv_credential_words: [] },
      administrator: recovery_administrator_account(),
      evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
    },
    controller: recovery_test_controller_observation(),
    password_write_policy: policy,
    operator_approval: recovery_operator_approval_declaration,
    reset_readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback_step,
  }
}

// WHAT converge IS HANDED: THE DECISIONS THE RUN OBTAINED FROM THE TWO GATES. They are parameters rather
// than results converge computes because the reset gate's admitting arm is NOT constructible at model time:
// it requires a UnitHoldAcquisition whose proof the hold module mints only from its own live acquires
// (UnitHoldProof is sole_constructor there, unit_hold_minted is admit_callers-gated). So no caller -- not
// even this module's own laws -- can manufacture a reset that the ledger admits with the unit held. That is
// the gate working, and the laws below assert exactly that at the refusals.
fn recovery_admitted_in_band_write() -> RecoveryWriteAuthorization {
  RecoveryWritePasswordAdmitted { authorized_by: recovery_in_band_password_write_site() }
}

fn recovery_admitted_reset_write() -> RecoveryWriteAuthorization {
  RecoveryWriteResetAdmitted {
    reset_type: ResetAll,
    authorized_by: recovery_manager_reset_site(),
    operator_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
  }
}

fn recovery_in_band_write_record() -> RecoveryWriteRecord {
  ExecutedWithoutByteCapture {
    kind: InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword,
    observed_exit: 0,
    detail: "operator-observed only; wiped by the reset before it could be re-read",
  }
}

fn recovery_reset_write_record() -> RecoveryWriteRecord {
  ExecutedWithoutByteCapture {
    kind: recovery_reset_actuation(),
    observed_exit: 0,
    detail: "observed in session; request and response bodies were not captured",
  }
}

fn recovery_factory_credential_readback() -> RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback {
  FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
    message_id: "Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired",
    evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_capture_instant,
  }
}
